  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "On reaction with sodium, 1 mol of a compound $\\mathrm{X}$ gives $1 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} .$ Which one of the following compounds might be $X ?$",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "bgCVb",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "xcQUb",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "1cpOi",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "93Xo8",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CH}_{2}(\\mathrm{OH}) \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Since 1 mole of compound Xreacts with Na to evolve 1 mole of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas, therefore the compound should have 2 active hydrogen atoms per mole which is possible only in option $d$.\r\n\r\n$\\begin{array}{l}\r\n\r\n\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OHCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}+2 \\mathrm{Na} \\\\\r\n\r\n\\longrightarrow \\mathrm{NaOCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{ONa}+2 \\mathrm{H}\r\n\r\n\\end{array}$",
    "image": null
  },
